COMMAND NAME: gpstate.sh

Shows the status of a running Greenplum Database system.

*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpstate.sh [-d <master_data_directory>] [-B <parallel_processes>] 
            [-s | -b] [-m] [-c] [-p] [-n] 
            {[-e] [-r <number>] [-g "<search_string>" [| "<search_string>"]]} 
            [-i] [-f] [-q] [-l <log_directory>] [-D] [-t | -? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpstate.sh script is analogous to functionality offered by the 
PostgreSQL pg_controldata command, which displays information about a 
running database server instance. There is additional information you 
may want to know about a Greenplum Database system, since it is comprised 
of multiple PostgreSQL database instances spanning multiple machines. 
The gpstate.sh script provides additional status information for a 
Greenplum Database system, such as:

* Which segments are down (invalid status).

* Master and segment configuration information (hosts, data directories, etc.)

* The ports used by the system.

* Segment log files.

* A mapping of primary segments to their corresponding mirror segments.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-d <master_data_directory>

Optional. The master data directory. If not specified, the value set for 
$MASTER_DATA_DIRECTORY will be used.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to check in parallel. If not specified, the script 
will start up to 60 parallel processes depending on how many segment 
instances it needs to check.


-s (status)

Optional. Displays detailed status information for the Greenplum Database 
system.


-b (brief status)

Optional. Display a brief summary of the state of the Greenplum Database 
system. This is the default option.


-m (list mirrors)

Optional. List the mirror segment instances in the system.


-c (show primary to mirror mappings)

Optional. Display mapping of primary segments to their corresponding mirror 
segments.


-p (show ports)

List the port numbers used throughout the Greenplum Database system.


-n (show log file names)

List the log file names for the master instance and each segment instance.


-e (show last lines of segment logs)

Display the end of the log file for the master instance and each segment 
instance.


-r number_of_log_lines

Used in combination with the -e option, retrieves the specified number of 
log lines. The default is to retrieve the last 10 lines of the log files.


-g <log_search_string>

Used in combination with the -e option, retrieves the log lines that match 
the specified search string. If a search string has spaces, enclose in quotes.
Use a pipe (|) to separate multiple search strings.


-i (show Greenplum Database version)

Display the Greenplum Database software version information.


-f (show standby master details)

Display details of the standby master host if configured.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is 
still written to the log file.


-l logfile_directory

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-t (show default script settings)

Display the default settings used by the gpstate.sh script such as the 
master data directory and port.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Show the complete status information of a Greenplum Database system:

gpstate.sh


Show the status summary of a Greenplum Database system:

gpstate.sh -b


Show information about mirror segment instances:

gpstate.sh -m -c


Show information about the standby master configuration:

gpstate.sh -f


Show the last log file message for all segment instances:

gpstate.sh -e


Display the online help for the gpstate.sh script:

gpstate.sh -?


